-- title:
--   Converting reachability into a short walk confined to a finite set
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a simple graph on a vertex type $V$, and let $S \subseteq V$ be **finite**. Let $r$ be a binary relation on $V$ such that
--
--   - $r(a,b)$ implies $a = b$ or $a$ is adjacent to $b$ in $G$;
--   - $r(a,b)$ implies $a \in S$ and $b \in S$.
--
--   If $a \in S$ and $b$ is reachable from $a$ by the reflexive-transitive closure of $r$, then there is a walk in $G$ from $a$ to $b$ which stays in $S$ and whose length is strictly less than the cardinality of $S$:
--
--   $$\exists\, w : a \rightsquigarrow b \ \text{ in } G, \qquad \operatorname{supp}(w) \subseteq S \quad\text{and}\quad \operatorname{length}(w) < \#S .$$
--
--   This strengthens the plain conversion of reachability into a walk by adding a quantitative length bound, which is what makes the walk usable in an argument that must control how many edges are consumed — the bound being the best possible of its kind, since a walk with no repeated vertex inside $S$ has at most $\#S$ vertices.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $V$ and $G$ are automatically bound implicit variables. The cardinality is `Set.ncard`, which is $0$ on infinite sets; the explicit finiteness hypothesis on $S$ makes it the genuine cardinality, and since $a \in S$ we have $\#S \ge 1$, so the bound is not vacuous.
